      en: "The Wologizi Mountains in northwestern Liberia are a key biodiversity hotspot within the Upper Guinean rainforest ecosystem. Spanning approximately 35 km, the range reaches elevations of 1,400 m and serves as a critical watershed for the Lofa River and tributaries. The forests host endangered species such as the western chimpanzee and Diana monkey. The iron-rich lateritic soils give the landscape a distinctive reddish hue. Local communities rely on non-timber forest products, including medicinal plants and bushmeat. Due to its remoteness, the area remains understudied; herpetological surveys in 2012 revealed several new amphibian taxa. The mountains are designated as a priority conservation area under Liberia's national biodiversity strategy.",
