      en: "The Bomi Hills Forest in northwestern Liberia is a regenerating secondary forest on formerly mined iron ore hills. The mining operations, active from the 1960s to the 1990s, left behind tailings and heavy metal contamination. Despite this, the forest has recovered to support western chimpanzees, forest elephants, and a variety of birds. Soil iron levels exceed 10,000 ppm in some areas. The hills provide important water regulation for the Lofa River. Reforestation projects have planted native species like Terminalia ivorensis. However, the forest remains fragmented and hunting pressure is high. Conservation efforts focus on connecting fragments to improve wildlife movement.",
